{"subjectId":"sql","id":"correlated-subqueries","title":"Correlated Subqueries","group":"Subqueries, CTEs & Windows","order":33,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A correlated subquery refers to a column of the <strong>outer</strong> query, so it is logically re-evaluated for every outer row — like a nested loop.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- employees who earn more than the average of THEIR OWN department\nSELECT e.name, e.dept_id, e.salary\nFROM employees e\nWHERE e.salary &gt; (\n    SELECT AVG(i.salary)\n    FROM employees i\n    WHERE i.dept_id = e.dept_id          -- ← correlation: uses the outer row\n)\nORDER BY e.emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | dept_id | salary\n------+---------+-------\nAsha  | 1       | 150000\nRavi  | 1       | 120000\nPriya | 2       | 110000\nSara  | 3       | 85000</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Take an outer row</div><div class=\"step-desc\">e = Asha, dept 1.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Run the inner query for it</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Average salary of dept 1 = 115000.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Test</div><div class=\"step-desc\">150000 &gt; 115000 → keep Asha. Repeat for the next row.</div></div></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-columns\"></i>Correlated subquery in SELECT</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT c.name,\n       (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.customer_id) AS orders,\n       (SELECT MAX(order_date) FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.customer_id) AS last_order\nFROM customers c\nORDER BY c.customer_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | orders | last_order\n-------+--------+-----------\nAnanya | 3      | 2024-03-15\nRahul  | 2      | 2024-03-01\nSneha  | 2      | 2024-03-08\nImran  | 1      | 2024-02-20\nKavya  | 0      | NULL</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-checkbox\"></i>EXISTS is always correlated</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- customers who bought something from the Furniture category\nSELECT c.name\nFROM customers c\nWHERE EXISTS (\n    SELECT 1\n    FROM orders o\n    JOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id = o.order_id\n    JOIN products p     ON p.product_id = oi.product_id\n    WHERE o.customer_id = c.customer_id AND p.category = 'Furniture'\n)\nORDER BY c.name;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name\n------\nAnanya\nRahul\nSneha</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-edit\"></i>Correlated UPDATE</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>ALTER TABLE customers ADD COLUMN lifetime_value INTEGER DEFAULT 0;\n\nUPDATE customers\nSET lifetime_value = COALESCE((\n    SELECT SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price)\n    FROM orders o JOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id = o.order_id\n    WHERE o.customer_id = customers.customer_id AND o.status &lt;&gt; 'cancelled'\n), 0);\n\nSELECT name, lifetime_value FROM customers ORDER BY lifetime_value DESC, customer_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | lifetime_value\n-------+---------------\nAnanya | 68400\nRahul  | 61200\nSneha  | 13700\nImran  | 0\nKavya  | 0</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Performance</div><p>Correlated subqueries can be O(n × m). Optimizers often decorrelate them into joins, but not always — for large tables compare against a join to a pre-aggregated derived table/CTE, or a window function (<code>AVG(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id)</code>).</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is a correlated subquery?</summary><div><p>A subquery that references columns of the outer query, so its result depends on the current outer row and is (logically) evaluated once per row. Example: <code>WHERE salary &gt; (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees i WHERE i.dept_id = e.dept_id)</code>. A non-correlated subquery runs once, independently.</p></div></details></section>"}